Z||000019||Taijiguy||07-08-2000||11:25 PM||||Yeah, I kind of have to agree with you GTBas. I've thrown alot of cash at my 85 GT, not to mention spent a buttload of time on my back skinning my knuckles on frozen not to mention inaccessable bolts. I think for someone to assume I'm summarily not "serious" just because I can't quote a bunch of numbers is pretty pretentious not to mention a little bit off-putting. I'd rather take that 130 bucks for the G-Tech, and put it into something ELSE to make it go faster or handle better. I don't really care dick about what the exact numbers are, as long as it puts me back in the seat when I mash the gas, gives me a view of the backwards-hat-rice-boy in my rear-view mirror, and allows me to at least DOUBLE the maximum safe speed around a corner, then I have all the answers I need about how fast it is!<BR>How fast is my Fiero? Damn fast, thanks for askin!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 07-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Eric||07-08-2000||11:46 PM||||Okay, put yourself in his shoes for just a second.  Shaun is throwing some serious money at his car.  He's building power the best way he knows how, with nitrous.  Now if someone put a 3.8 in his car, and said it was the fastest thing out there for the dough and he had real numbers to back it up, maybe Shaun would be building up a 3.8 instead.  I know throughout my 3.1 project I tried to ask others what kind of concrete performance numbers the achieved with their stroked motors and I got no real response.  Since I have completed my project I have been very honest with others who want to do the same thing and that usually includes saying, "If I had to do it over again, I would have gone with the 3.4."  So just ease back people.||||reg||1
